we've recently covered quite a few  deities associated of death and it's  extremely interesting to see how  different cultures attempt to approach  and explain the natural event in Greek  mythology we see the presence of  Thanatos as almost welcomed because he  symbolizes the gentle and painless  release of death in Egypt Anubis is  somewhat of an escort ensuring that the  dead passed safely into the afterlife  but in Japan they have their own twist  on death the Shinigami meaning gods of  death or death spirits  rather than being directly responsible  and someone dies they must invite an  usher people to their own demise  the term Shinigami isn't one that  appears too much Japanese literature and  it doesn't apply to a specific God or  spirit but rather to a variety of  entities that are believed to bring  death describing their appearance can be  rather difficult as they can take a host  of different forms appearing as a god  spirit or demon like figure in early  Japanese literature the Shinigami will  never really describe this physical  entities if we look at Ningaloo or yuri  or Bunraku the ancient Japanese art of  puppet theatre the works of chicken  matzo monzaemon commonly tackled the  issue of suicide in a particular story  he describes a man and a woman  attempting to commit a double suicide  they described life has been extremely  short moon being lowered by a death God  it's unclear if their actions were  driven by the existence of a Shinigami  but in this context the word Shinigami  was almost used as an expression to  describe how short life can be  especially when we waste time worrying  about the inevitable end that awaits us  all in the classical literature of the  18th and 19th century the depictions of  the Shinigami began to change if we take  a look at the Aeon Haku monogatari often  referred to as the picture book of a  hundred stories it covers a host of  different yokai and supernatural  entities there is a story titled  Shinigami and in this story the  Shinigami is described as the spirit of  someone who passed but still harbored  bad intent quite similar to how we would  describe a ghost or a poltergeist the  spirit would lead the living along a bad  path and take them to places where  previous steps had occurred they were  known to lead those who were  contemplating suicide to places where  others had previously hung themselves in  the hope that history would repeat it  of this form of Shinigami was believed  to possess people and slowly break their  will  causing them to want to die in later  years it was believed that several  things would happen to someone who was  possessed by this particular type of  spirit they would either find it  extremely difficult to tell the truth  when asked questions or find it almost  impossible to speak at all we can almost  assume that these are techniques used by  the Shinigami to alienate their hosts  from friends and family making it much  harder for them to seek help it's  believed that they could invade a  person's thoughts forcing them to relive  every horrible thing they had ever done  until it became so much that death was  the only release the Izanagi  and the Izanami are what many consider  to be examples of the very first  Shinigami they were the eight pairs of  brother and sister deities that appeared  when the earth was created and they were  often associated with both creation and  death the Shinigami are quite similar to  the other colony of the Shinto religion  but their history doesn't stretch as far  back because after all they are  relatively recent invention  it wasn't until Japan came into contact  with the West and their ideas of  particular gods associated of death that  the idea of the Shinigami really began  to grow and develop where it was common  for Western cultures to have very few  cards associated of death in Japan there  were many Shinigami and they often  worked in groups and pairs there was an  interesting tale about a man who is fed  up with his life and so he contemplates  suicide but before he is able to do so  he is visited by Shinigami  who tells him that his time has not come  yet the spirit explained to the man that  each person's life is measured on the  candle and once the flame burns out that  person dies these depictions differ  drastically and contradict the early  depictions of the Shinigami who appeared  more as vengeful spirits it's very clear  that in this story they have no control  over who lives and who dies they're  essentially told when a person's time  has come to an end  but perhaps they are even able to sense  when a person's time is near when the  figurative candle is about to burn out  to stop the man end in his life the  Shinigami tells him how he could become  very rich the man would pretend to be a  doctor who would claim he could cure any  disease he was given a magical phrase of  words that would send any Shinigami back  to the underworld and thus lengthen  someone's life the phrase would only  work if the Shinigami was sitting at the  foot of someone's bed  if they were at the head of the bed the  magical phrase would only cause a sick  person to die with his newfound  knowledge the man indeed grew very rich  and one day when visiting a patient he  saw the Shinigami at the head of the  patients bed the family begged the man  to help and offer the client in several  offers the family offered an amount of  money so large that the man was consumed  by greed when the Shinigami fell asleep  the patient swapped the ends of the bed  and the magical words sent the spirit  back to the underworld when the man  arrived home the same Shinigami was  waiting for him infuriated of what he  had just done the Shinigami told the man  that all would be forgotten if they went  for a drink to celebrate the man's newly  earned fortune the man of course agreed  not knowing that he had just fallen for  a trick he was taken to a building  filled with candles and he was shown his  own Candle which was nearly burnt out  because of his actions  the man was told he could extend his  life by transferring the wick and the  wax of his candle to another but of  course this was another trick the man  failed dropping his candle and dying  this is a story that I really enjoy  because not only does it show us that  the Shinigami are unpredictable but they  seem to have their own distinct  personalities the first Shinigami  appeared as quite friendly explaining to  the man how death worked and even  helping him improve his life the  following spirit said the man came  across did not seem to be too concerned  of the fact that he was sending them  back to the underworld but it was a lost  Shinigami he was angered by his actions  swear in revenge against the man and  ultimately taken the man's life as a  payment for the soul he had stopped him  acquiring now I know you fans of the  Death Note anime and manga had been  waiting for this video for a while and a  video discussed in the Shinigami  wouldn't exactly be complete without  taking and look at their recent  depictions and transformations I had  previously never read or watched if no  but I had heard of the name and it  wasn't until watching the Netflix movie  adaptation that I even came across the  Shinigami the Shinigami appear as demon  like gods who write the names of mortals  in books known as death notes causing  them to die in the sixth end their own  life Shinigami are not responsible for  every death that occurs but they can  sometimes end their life sooner than  intended  the character known as Ryoga is quite a  curious Shinigami who out of boredom  drops one of these said death notes in  to the human world with instructions on  how it can be used those who find the  book are essentially entered into a  bargain or a pact with Ryoga where he  agrees to kill anyone whose name is  written in the book in the desired  manner  Ryoga appears to feel no apathy for  those who find the book as they  essentially just the means for him to  extend his own life but he doesn't  mention several times that he finds  humans and their behavior extremely  interesting the individuals who enter  into an agreement of react are told if  they no longer desire to be the keeper  of the book then they can pass it along  to someone else or they can separate  themselves from the book for seven days  once the seven days are up Ryo could  find a new person to assume the role as  keeper of the book those who find the  book are often consumed by the power it  comes with and ultimately the Death Note  is responsible for their own death the  Shinigami in Death Note do also vary in  appearance if we take react as an  example he has a long thin appendages  and a ghoulish like appearance he's head  and neck appeared to be stitched to his  body and he almost reminds me of Jack  Skellington from The Nightmare Before  Christmas only much more menacing and  deadly there are quite a few Shinigami  that appear in the series and the ones  similarity and appearance that they all  seem to share is that they will resemble  corpses and I guess when you think about  it that isn't too out of the ordinary  for gods of death if these depictions of  the Shinigami are something that  interests you then I suggest you either  check out the enemy of the manga or  perhaps even the Netflix movie if bad  movies are something you enjoy the  deities surrounding death in other  cultures are often shown to be selfless  individuals who perform a thankless task  I guess one of the main reasons I find  the Shinigami so interesting is because  they differ drastically not only because  of the sheer amount of them that exist  but they seem to have their own distinct  personalities the role that the  Shinigami played drastically changed  over the years in Japan in early  Japanese culture they were used to  explain someone dying particularly the  act of suicide but they soon began to  manifest as vengeful spirits who were  capable of possessing people and driving  them insane until the early release was  there  in the most recent depictions they  appear as demon like gods who strike  bargains of mortals as a means of  extending their own lives the Shinigami  are most definitely a mixed bag there  are those who choose to help people as  they transition into the underworld  there are those who seek revenge and  enjoy causing death and there are those  who use the lives of mortals as  bargaining tools as a means to extend  their own existence whether you see the  Shinigami as spirits demons or gods  they're extremely intriguing figures who  give us a different outlook on deities  surrounding death  once again I'd like to thank all the  patrons who voted on this week's video  and for all the support you've given me  if you guys interested in becoming a  patron and having a choice in one of the  videos I make every week then I'll leave  a link in the description as well as an  end screen that you guys can click on  but as always I've been your host  mythology and fiction explained  [Music]
 

